{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. \n*Early Life* \nhe was born around 1140 and became the Count of Tripoli after his father's death in 1152. Raymond III was still a minor when he became the Count and the regency was exercised by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. \n*Reign* \nRaymond III played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during his reign he was a strong supporter of the Haute Cour and opposed the monarch's attempts to centralize power. He also clashed with King Amalric of Jerusalem over the issue of the monarch's marriage to Agnes of Courtenay. \n*Relationships and Alliances* \nRaymond III formed an alliance with the Templars and the Hospitallers to counterbalance the power of the monarch he also formed an alliance with the Sultan of Damascus to counter the threat posed by the Sultan of Aleppo. Raymond III was captured by the Sultan of Aleppo in 1164 and was held prisoner for several years before being released in 1170. \n*Later Life and Death* \nafter his release from captivity Raymond III continued to play an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem he was a strong supporter of the Count of Flanders and opposed the attempts of the monarch to centralize power. Raymond III died on September 29 1187 without leaving a male heir the County of Tripoli was inherited by his godson Raymond of Antioch. \n*Legacy* \nRaymond III is remembered as a skilled diplomat and strategist who played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during his reign he was a strong supporter of the nobility and opposed the attempts of the monarch to centralize power. Raymond III also formed alliances with the Templars and the Hospitallers to counterbalance the power of the monarch and formed an alliance w